<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/lightning-sky.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/lightning-sky-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/lightning-sky.webp 956w" sizes="100vw" width="956" height="538" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">›</span><a href="/claim-notebook/">Claim notebook</a><span aria-hidden="true">›</span>Hurricane Deductibles in Rhode Island: How the Math Works</nav><p class="tag">Claim notebook</p><h1>Hurricane Deductibles in Rhode Island: How the Math Works</h1><p class="lede">Why a 2% deductible can mean $10,000 or more, what usually triggers it and why the full wind scope matters even more.</p></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Rhode Island Insurance Appraiser team · <time datetime="2026-10-21">October 21, 2026</time> · 6 min read</p>
<p>If you own a home near Rhode Island’s coast, and often even if you don’t, your policy probably has a hurricane or named-storm deductible. It’s one of the most misunderstood parts of a property claim.</p>
<h2>A percentage of coverage, not of the loss</h2>
<p>A hurricane deductible is typically 1% to 5% of your dwelling coverage (Coverage A). On a home insured for $450,000, a 2% deductible is $9,000. If wind damage totals $14,000, the policy pays $5,000. If the damage totals $8,500, the policy pays nothing.</p>
<h2>What triggers it</h2>
<p>The policy spells out the trigger. Common versions refer to a hurricane or named storm declared by the National Hurricane Center or National Weather Service, sometimes with a window of hours before and after the storm. A strong nor’easter normally isn’t a named storm, so the standard deductible usually applies.</p>
<h2>Why the full scope matters even more</h2>
<p>With a fixed deductible of several thousand dollars, every missed slope, siding wall or interior ceiling comes straight out of what you’re paid. That’s why a careful estimate review matters most when a hurricane deductible is in play.</p>
<h3>Try your numbers</h3>
<p>Our <a href="/policyholder-tools/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> converts the percentage into dollars and compares it with your standard deductible. All figures in this article are illustrative.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="related"><a href="/claim-notebook/documenting-ice-dam-and-nor-easter-damage/"><small>Guide</small><span>Documenting Ice Dam and Nor’easter Damage for a Claim</span><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/claim-notebook/wind-versus-flood-after-a-coastal-storm/"><small>Guide</small><span>Wind Versus Flood After a Coastal Storm</span><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/claim-notebook/matching-materials-on-older-rhode-island-homes/"><small>Guide</small><span>Matching Materials on Older Rhode Island Homes</span><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-t">Talk it through</h2><p>A free first conversation. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="box-phone" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ink" href="/request-a-claim-review/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-t">Learn</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/the-appraisal-sequence/">The appraisal sequence<svg class="ic" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" strok